so that an AI agent can implement the change without human clarification.

STORY: __STORY_KEY__ — __STORY_TITLE__

DESCRIPTION:
__STORY_DESCRIPTION__

Rules:
1. Each AC must be specific and testable (observable in UI, API response, or logs).
2. Infer "expected" behaviour from the description, domain knowledge, and the word "NOT".
3. Include: primary success path, at least one edge case, and one error/boundary case.
4. Do NOT invent requirements not implied by the description.
5. Use plain English, present tense, third-person ("The system...", "The UI shows...").
6. Describe WHAT to verify (observable behavior), never HOW to implement it. Do NOT prescribe a mechanism/algorithm — no "calculate independently", "split", "halve", "per segment", "for each line item", new fields, or internal approach. Keep a bug's ACs at the symptom/behavior level; prescribing an implementation misdirects the downstream code investigation.

Project context — this pipeline has two TypeScript codelines, both ESM (`"type": "module"`) with vitest and tsc:

- mocka (`src/fares.ts`) — transit fare logic. Exports `fareFor(rider: Rider): number` and `totalFor(riders: Rider[]): number`. A `Rider` is `{ age: number; hasPass: boolean }`. Fares are integer cents (`BASE_FARE_CENTS`, `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS`). Tests live in `test/fares.test.ts` and use vitest `describe`/`it`/`expect` with direct value assertions (e.g. `expect(fareFor({ age: 30, hasPass: false })).toBe(BASE_FARE_CENTS)`).
- mockb (`src/schedule.ts`) — departure-board rendering. Exports `formatStops(stops: Stop[]): string` and `totalMinutes(stops: Stop[]): number`. A `Stop` is `{ name: string; minutes: number }`. `formatStops` joins stops with `' -> '`. Tests live in `test/schedule.test.ts` using the same vitest idiom.

Both codelines run tests with `npm test` (vitest run) and type-check with `npm run build` (tsc --noEmit). There is no lint step.

When writing ACs, express expected behaviour in terms of the observable return values of these exported functions (fare amounts in cents, formatted stop strings). The downstream agent will verify against vitest assertions, so each AC should be expressible as a concrete `expect(...).toBe(...)` or `expect(...).toContain(...)` check.

Respond with JSON only — no markdown, no preamble:
{ "enrichedAcs": ["<AC 1>", "<AC 2>", ...] }
